Steam Deck: send the trackpads' haptic pulses as a joystick effect

SDL's Steam Deck driver plays rumble on the trackpads' actuators but
refuses SDL_SendGamepadEffect. With this change the driver accepts one
kind of effect: a 65-byte buffer, the report ID byte followed by the
controller's 64-byte feature report, whose report type is the trackpad
haptic command (ID_TRIGGER_HAPTIC_CMD). It sends that report to the
controller with SDL_hid_send_feature_report, as the Steam Controller
driver sends its own effects. Every other size or report type stays
unsupported, so nothing else the driver does changes.

Open Annihilation applies it to the pinned SDL release when
tools/bootstrap_sdl.py unpacks the release, so that the game can play a
light tick on one trackpad; without it the game plays the same feel as
rumble. The change is offered upstream to SDL, under SDL's own licence
(zlib), the terms of the file it changes.

--- a/src/joystick/hidapi/SDL_hidapi_steamdeck.c
+++ b/src/joystick/hidapi/SDL_hidapi_steamdeck.c
@@ -462,7 +462,17 @@
 
 static bool HIDAPI_DriverSteamDeck_SendJoystickEffect(SDL_HIDAPI_Device *device, SDL_Joystick *joystick, const void *data, int size)
 {
-    return SDL_Unsupported();
+    // A trackpad haptic command: the report ID byte, then the controller's
+    // feature report, whose first byte is the report type.
+    const Uint8 *report = (const Uint8 *)data;
+
+    if (!report || size != HID_FEATURE_REPORT_BYTES + 1 || report[1] != ID_TRIGGER_HAPTIC_CMD) {
+        return SDL_Unsupported();
+    }
+    if (SDL_hid_send_feature_report(device->dev, report, size) != size) {
+        return SDL_SetError("Couldn't send the trackpad haptic report");
+    }
+    return true;
 }
 
 static bool HIDAPI_DriverSteamDeck_SetSensorsEnabled(SDL_HIDAPI_Device *device, SDL_Joystick *joystick, bool enabled)
